1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,033
Stockroom tracks workshop parts, shelf locations and every stock movement.

2
00:00:05,033 --> 00:00:11,433
Search narrows the inventory by item name or SKU.

3
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:18,800
Stock filters separate low inventory from items that are completely out of stock.

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,600
New items start at zero stock. Receipts and issues change the balance through the ledger.

5
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:31,433
A Torx bit set is added with a unique SKU, a shelf location and a reorder threshold.

6
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:34,867
A receipt records both the quantity and its reference.

7
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:42,233
Receiving five units updates the balance and creates a permanent movement record.

8
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:47,000
The item detail connects its current balance to its transaction history.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,967
Item details can change without rewriting the stock history.

10
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:57,600
The shelf changes from D-04 to D-05; the five-unit balance stays unchanged.

11
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,433
An issue removes units and records the reason. The resulting balance is shown before saving.

12
00:01:01,433 --> 00:01:08,800
All five units are issued to the workshop, bringing the item back to zero.

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,533
Only a zero-stock item can be archived. Its receipts and issues remain recorded.

14
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:17,133
The item is archived after its stock reaches zero.

15
00:01:17,133 --> 00:01:23,600
Archiving removes the item from active inventory without deleting its history.

16
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:29,733
FastAPI publishes the REST endpoints and request schemas through OpenAPI.

17
00:01:29,733 --> 00:01:36,067
POST creates a zero-stock item with a unique SKU.

18
00:01:36,067 --> 00:01:40,867
GET returns the newly stored item.

19
00:01:40,867 --> 00:01:46,700
PUT updates the shelf location and increments the item version.

20
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:51,533
DELETE archives the zero-stock item and returns HTTP 204.

21
00:01:51,533 --> 00:01:58,500
PostgreSQL stores inventory items, movement history and schema migration records.

22
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:05,367
All 21 API and concurrency tests pass against an isolated PostgreSQL database.

23
00:02:05,367 --> 00:02:14,000
Build and start the application containers with Docker Compose.

24
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,533
Frontend, API and PostgreSQL are healthy after the build.

25
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:25,567
The search description identifies the query but does not show the number of matches.

26
00:02:25,567 --> 00:02:35,567
Show the number of search matches and check both singular and zero-result wording.

27
00:02:35,567 --> 00:02:45,333
Commit the search-count change and push it to GitHub.

28
00:02:45,333 --> 00:02:50,333
The pushed commit starts the GitHub Actions workflow.

29
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:56,467
The push starts tests and infrastructure checks before image publishing.

30
00:02:56,467 --> 00:03:02,733
Tests, infrastructure checks, browser tests and image publication all passed for commit 99a0e2c.

31
00:03:02,733 --> 00:03:09,467
The backend Trivy report identifies the published image and reports zero HIGH/CRITICAL findings.

32
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:15,533
The frontend image also passes the HIGH/CRITICAL vulnerability gate.

33
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:21,567
Both published GHCR images carry the full source commit SHA.

34
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:25,567
Upgrade the application to the exact frontend and backend images published by this commit.

35
00:03:25,567 --> 00:03:30,367
Helm revision 5 is deployed successfully.

36
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:37,367
Helm lists revision 5; both Deployments use the newly published commit.

37
00:03:37,367 --> 00:03:44,833
The updated search now displays “1 match for Arduino” through the Kubernetes ingress.

38
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:51,967
Kubernetes reports healthy frontend and backend pods, Services and ingress.

39
00:03:51,967 --> 00:03:57,900
Terraform defines the VPC and public/private subnets across two availability zones.

40
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:04,500
EKS enables audit logs and restricts public API access to configured CIDRs.

41
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:09,533
The EKS cluster runs in Mumbai.

42
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:14,667
The managed node group contains two worker nodes.

43
00:04:14,667 --> 00:04:20,700
The API exports request counters and latency histograms in Prometheus format.

44
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:27,267
The HPA increases backend capacity under load.

45
00:04:27,267 --> 00:04:35,267
Grafana records request rate, latency, zero server errors and scaling from two replicas to five.

46
00:04:35,267 --> 00:04:41,700
Prometheus scrapes the application replicas successfully.

47
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:47,900
Apply an invalid container image and a Service with an incorrect selector.

48
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:55,067
The invalid image enters ImagePullBackOff, and the mismatched Service has no endpoints.

49
00:04:55,067 --> 00:05:03,067
Correct the image tag and the Service selector.

50
00:05:03,067 --> 00:05:09,733
Both pods are Ready, and the repaired Service has a matching endpoint.

51
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:15,600
The saved AWS plan added 34 resources; the teardown removed all 34.

52
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:23,567
The EKS cluster list is empty after Terraform destroys the environment.
